WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.520
What has happened in Chemistry is that

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.420
there has been this inertia

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:03.880
of people doing chemistry

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.680
the same way for hundreds of years

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.069
because
it's - this is a model of apprenticeship,

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:10.620
the advisor tells us you know, how to do things.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.900
The student doesn't really question

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.820
if that's the right way

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:13.469
of doing it or not.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:15.520
So what we're doing in this program is saying

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.340
well, wait a second,

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.119
do you mean the 4th Industrial

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:19.119
Revolution?

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:20.560
We have all these new tools

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.880
especially artificial
intelligence and robotics

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.140
that can transform

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.630
the way we do science.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.340
So functionally we are

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.980
going to change the
scientific discovery process

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.680
you know?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.000
This is not about the simple hypothesis

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.340
driven research,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.800
loops of iteration.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.640
It's about fundamentally challenging

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.900
how we go about the scientific

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.960
discovery process

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:39.969
in chemical space.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:45.680
It is believed that you know, developing a
new material takes about $10 million in 10 years

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.160
roughly, order of magnitude right?

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.380
It is because all this chemistry is done by
hand and is done in a very time inefficient fashion.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.200
Several layers of science have been transformed
by the use of machinery.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.500
The focus of what we're talking here is really
building out the tools, platforms, and methods

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:07.290
of how we go into chemical discovery, but
that’s the thing, chemistry sits as a central

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:13.970
science to almost anything; health, materials,
energy, so we're talking about not only changing

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.500
how one goes and does discovery in chemical
science, but the feedback, the spill off from

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:25.420
that means we can affect things like energy
storage, energy conversion from solar, new

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.450
pharmaceutical drugs, new textiles.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:34.680
One of the things that we want is that any
educated or trained person that has a molecular

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.380
dream, that imagines a potential solution
to a problem in the world has easy access

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:48.260
to a machine that we could call it like a
molecular maker lab that you write what molecule

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.820
you want and it will tell you what are the
reagents that you need, it will put them together,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.650
make small quantities of this material and
then go and test it.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.400
You know, people new to the CIFAR program
don't really know what to make of it, you know,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.020
it's the first time they've heard of it.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.340
The thing that's enabling about this is it's
allowing us to break down borders and really

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.870
put many different people in the same room
and giving them free reign to discuss.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:16.600
You know, this is giving people the opportunity
to make these sort of connections, long-standing

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.960
connections and also again it provides a flag
for people to rally around that we are CIFAR fellows.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.320
[Music]

